{
  "dom_id": "step-q143388",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ for $y\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent",
  "worked_solution": "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements is true.\"",
